complete(identifier,dict(
 title='Sunflower conjecture',status='source_open',criterion=old['criterion'],question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''For every integer \(r\ge3\), does there exist a real constant \(C_r\ge1\) such that, for every integer \(k\ge1\), every finite set \(X\), and every family \(\mathcal F\subseteq\{S\subseteq X:|S|=k\}\),
\[
|\mathcal F|>C_r^k\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\exists\text{ distinct }S_1,\ldots,S_r\in\mathcal F\ \exists K\subseteq X\ \forall i\ne j:\ S_i\cap S_j=K?
\]
The constant may depend on \(r\) but not on the set size \(k\), the ground set or the family. The conclusion is the existence of an ordinary \(r\)-sunflower.''',
 definitions=r'''A family here is a finite set of subsets, so identical members are not repeated. It is \(k\)-uniform when every member has exactly \(k\) elements. There is no bound on the finite ground-set size \(|X|\). An \(r\)-sunflower consists of \(r\) pairwise distinct members whose pairwise intersections are all the same set \(K\), called the core. For \(r\ge3\), this core also equals the intersection of all selected members. Removing \(K\) leaves pairwise disjoint petals. The core may be empty, in which case the selected sets are pairwise disjoint. For distinct equal-sized members their petals are nonempty.

The quantifier order is \(\forall r\,\exists C_r\,\forall k,X,\mathcal F\). No single base independent of \(r\) is requested. One may equivalently require an integer \(C_r\ge2\), since a larger integer base preserves the upper bound. An eventual bound for all sufficiently large \(k\), with the threshold depending on \(r\), also yields the displayed all-\(k\) formulation by enlarging \(C_r\) to cover finitely many small sizes.

The classical sunflower lemma bounds the size of any sunflower-free \(k\)-uniform family by \(k!(r-1)^k\), independently of its ground set. Consequently the largest such size \(S_r(k)\) exists as a finite integer, even though the ground set is unrestricted. The conjecture is equivalently \(S_r(k)\le C_r^k\) for all \(k\). It does not ask for the exact value, a numerical estimate or constant-factor asymptotics of \(S_r(k)\).

The source also states the conjecture for sets of size at most \(k\). Padding every smaller set to size \(k\) with fresh elements unique to that member preserves pairwise intersections and reduces that variant to the displayed one. This observation is not a claim about robust sunflowers: their probabilistic coverage requirement is stronger, and lower bounds for that stronger structure do not refute the ordinary conjecture.

There is no computational requirement on finding the sunflower or computing the constants. This is a quantified extremal-combinatorial proposition, so the numerical absolute-error convention does not replace it.''',
 answer_criterion=r'''Give a complete mathematically correct Lean-checked proof of the displayed conjecture or its negation. An affirmative proof must establish a finite base for every fixed \(r\ge3\). A negative proof must supply one fixed \(r\ge3\) for which every candidate base \(C\ge1\) admits a \(k\)-uniform family of more than \(C^k\) members with no \(r\)-sunflower. Improving known superconstant bases, proving only the case of one petal count, or disproving a robust-sunflower variant does not decide the full statement.''',
 source_formulation=dict(text='Conjecture 1.3 asks for a constant-base exponential bound for each fixed number of petals. The present review uses that binary source conjecture as an announced editorial default under the user’s later preference for substantive conjectures, replacing the September 12 broader optimal-function request without claiming a new explicit user confirmation.',citation='primary',caption='Alweiss–Lovett–Wu–Zhang, Conjecture 1.3; Rao §§1–1.1.',format='editorial_paraphrase'),
 formulation_history=(old.get('formulation_history') or [])+[dict(recorded_on='2026-09-18',formal=old['formal'],answer_criterion=old['answer_criterion'],reason='Saved September 12 constant-factor extremal-function target before the announced return to the source conjecture under the later binary-target policy.')],
 references=refs,
 context_blocks=[block('The classical lemma forces a sunflower only after a factorial-sized threshold. The conjecture would replace this by an exponential whose base is independent of the set size.','survey'),block('Modern bounds remove most of the classical growth but still have a base growing logarithmically with the set size. The robust sunflower method has its own obstruction at that scale, which does not settle ordinary sunflowers.','survey'),block('Sunflower structure supports computational lower bounds and other combinatorial reductions. A uniform exponential threshold would strengthen the general principle used in such applications.','survey')],
 progress=[progress('2020',r'The primary breakthrough gives bounds with a base polylogarithmic in the uniformity, improving the classical factorial threshold while retaining Conjecture 1.3.','primary'),progress('2026',r'Rao’s journal survey, in the checked September 2025 author manuscript, states the conjecture as open and surveys an \(O(r\log k)^k\) bound. It distinguishes the necessary logarithmic dependence for robust sunflowers from the ordinary target.','survey')],
),['Selected the source binary exponential-bound conjecture as an announced editorial default after an optional scope question; no user confirmation is asserted.','Preserved the superseded September 12 asymptotic target in formulation history.','Defined all quantifiers, distinct sets, empty core, uniformity and unrestricted finite ground sets.','Distinguished ordinary and robust sunflowers and retained individual importance 89.'],['Read the primary Alweiss et al. Definition 1.1, Conjecture 1.3, Theorem 1.4 and robust-sunflower discussion.','Read Rao’s author survey dated September 9, 2025, §§1–1.1, and checked its 2026 journal metadata.','Bounded later-work searches through 18 September 2026 found restricted-intersection and vector-space variants, but no verified ordinary sunflower-conjecture resolution.'],'The ordinary exponential-bound conjecture is explicitly retained in the checked Rao survey, published in 2026 with a September 2025 author manuscript. Robust-sunflower barriers and results for restricted families do not decide it. Bounded later checks through 18 September 2026 are not an exhaustive certification.',summary=['A sunflower is a collection of distinct sets with one common pairwise intersection.','The conjecture fixes the number of petals and asks how many equal-sized sets force that structure.','It asks for an exponential threshold whose base depends only on the petal count.','The ground set is any finite set, and the core may be empty.','Known logarithmic-base bounds and limitations for robust sunflowers leave the ordinary exponential conjecture open in the checked sources.'],expected_sha256=claim['input_sha256'],claim_token=claim['token'])
